<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-electric-cars">
  <title>Charging electric cars</title>

  <epigraph>
    <q>We all make mistakes, and it is better to make them before we begin.</q>
    <attribution>Nikola Tesla</attribution>
  </epigraph>

  <introduction>
    <p><em>Background.</em> A study was carried out in 2014-15 of how electric car drivers used charging facilities provided by a firm participating in the U.S. Department of Energy workplace charging challenge.</p>
    <p><em>Questions.</em> How often did users charge their cars? When did they charge them? How long did they charge them for? Which charging stations were used most? Which incentives work in controlling use of charging stations?</p>
    <p><em>Sources.</em> @asensio:2021a and @asensio:2021b</p>
    <p><em>Structure.</em> 3395 charging sessions with 24 variables were reported, including user, location, time, charge, and cost.</p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="secElecC1">
    <title>How was the study organised?</title>

    <p>The hierarchical structure of the study was that there were four types of company facility, each with a number of locations, and different numbers of charging stations at the locations. Distances driven and use of other charging options were not recorded. Financial and other incentives were employed to reduce congestion and waiting, though there is no information on what congestion and waiting actually occurred.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-elecEns">
      <caption>Number of chargings carried out by user, by facility type, by location, and by charging station (locations and charging stations are coloured by the type of facility where they were located).</caption>
      <image source="images/elecEns-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>Figure <xref ref="fig-elecEns"/> is an ensemble of plots summarising some of the data. A few users charged many times and around half charged hardly at all. Most locations were at R&amp;D facilities, as were most stations. One Manufacturing location was used more than any other. One Office station was used far more often than other Office stations. Some stations and even a few locations were hardly used.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-elecLocs">
      <caption>Numbers of charging stations by facility type and location.</caption>
      <image source="images/elecLocs-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>Figure <xref ref="fig-elecLocs"/> shows the 25 locations by facility type and number of stations. Locations are sorted by facility type and within type by station count.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-elecChU">
      <caption>Numbers of charges per day with smooth added.</caption>
      <image source="images/elecChU-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>The numbers of charges over the period are shown in Figure <xref ref="fig-elecChU"/>. Sharp dips are probably weekends.</p>

    <p>The study lasted about a year from mid-November 2014 to early October 2015. The researchers state in their article (@asensio:2021a) that the first three months were a testing period and the remaining nine months were the analysis period. Their code shows testing began on 18 November 2014 and ended on 18 February 2015. Charging stations were installed over time, so not all could be used over the full analysis period.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-elecStatts">
      <caption>Numbers of charging stations by FacilityType over the study.</caption>
      <image source="images/elecStatts-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>Throughout the study there were always more stations available at R&amp;D facilities than elsewhere. Several Office stations were only added late in the study.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-elecStat">
      <caption>When charging stations were used during the study.</caption>
      <image source="images/elecStat-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>Figure <xref ref="fig-elecStat"/> shows when stations were used. The default plot shows little use early on, some lightly used stations, and a group only used late in the study.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-elecU">
      <caption>When users charged their cars, sorted by number of charges.</caption>
      <image source="images/elecU-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>Patterns of user behaviour can be seen in Figure <xref ref="fig-elecU"/>. Most heavy users charged occasionally first and then more regularly.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-elecStat2">
      <caption>Use of charging stations, coloured by facility type, grouped by location, ordered by first installation at location and by first date of use, with the end of testing marked in red.</caption>
      <image source="images/elecStat2-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>Figure <xref ref="fig-elecStat2"/> groups stations by location, orders by first use, and marks the end of testing in red. Some locations had few stations and were barely used. One Manufacturing location was heavily used while another was hardly used. Two later R&amp;D locations were hardly used. It would be informative to know why usage varied so much.</p>

    <p>The analysis period appears to be only seven and a half months, not the nine months reported in the paper. Overall, the lack of balance in the study is surprising, and the evolving charging environment likely influenced behaviour.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="secElecC2">
    <title>How long does charging cars take?</title>

    <p>Charging was free for up to four hours, after that there was a one dollar per hour fee. Staff were recommended not to connect for more than two hours at a time. Figure <xref ref="fig-elecCh"/> shows charging times with two-hour and four-hour limits marked. One outlying charging time over 55 hours is excluded, as are 55 sessions with zero charge.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-elecCh">
      <caption>Distribution of charging time in hours.</caption>
      <image source="images/elecCh-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>Either cars were fully charged within four hours or many users avoided paying fees. There appears to be no drop at two hours. There are a few very short times.</p>

    <p>Plotting charge against charging time gives the surprising pattern in Figure <xref ref="fig-elecChT"/>. For much data there is a rising linear pattern from 0 to 2 hours, which then flattens. There are cases where charging appears highly effective, less effective, or barely effective. The horizontal boundary from two hours onward could indicate cars fully charged but left connected.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-elecChT">
      <caption>Energy charge in kilowatt-hours by charging time in hours.</caption>
      <image source="images/elecChT-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>Most cases above 10 kWh are due to a small subset of users, as shown in Figure <xref ref="fig-elecChTc"/>.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-elecChTc">
      <caption>Energy charge in kilowatt-hours by charging time in hours, where charges higher than 10 kWh have been coloured by the user ID (charges less than 10 kWh for those users have not been coloured).</caption>
      <image source="images/elecChTc-1.png"/>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="secElecC3">
    <title>Charging patterns through the week</title>

    <p>Figure <xref ref="fig-elecScats"/> shows scatterplots of total kilowatt charge by charging time by day of week and facility type, excluding charging times over six hours and sessions at the two Other locations.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-elecScats">
      <caption>Energy charge in kilowatt-hours by charging time in hours, grouped by day of the week and type of facility, excluding charging times over six hours. The barchart shows the number of charges by day.</caption>
      <image source="images/elecScats-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>There were few charging sessions at weekends. There were more sessions at R&amp;D locations than elsewhere. The scatterplots suggest some limit around 7 kWh, and R&amp;D includes a few bigger weekday charges. A lower boundary suggests at least a certain time is needed for a given charge. The study omitted 55 sessions with zero charging even though charge times were not negligible.</p>

    <p><em>Answers.</em> About half the users charged their cars rarely, if at all. Cars were seldom charged at weekends. Some locations were not used much. Users generally charged for less than four hours, after which they had to pay a fee.</p>
    <p><em>Further questions.</em> Do charging patterns change over time? When did users start and stop charging during the day? What percentage of time were stations occupied?</p>

    <p><em>Graphical takeaways.</em></p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Initial exploratory graphics suggest questions to follow up. (Figures <xref ref="fig-elecEns"/> to <xref ref="fig-elecStat"/>)</p></li>
      <li><p>Colour, separating space, and nested ordering are valuable for distinguishing groups. (Figure <xref ref="fig-elecStat2"/>)</p></li>
      <li><p>Ensembles of graphics are effective at pulling information together. (Figures <xref ref="fig-elecEns"/> and <xref ref="fig-elecScats"/>)</p></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</chapter>
